Loaded playbook: consolidate_findings
Description: Sweep notes, per-phase reports, phase logs and artifacts on disk for every finding produced so far, normalise them into one list, and record them to notes so the next phase receives a complete input. Gathers only - does not test, does not chase new leads.
Mode: agent

╭─────────────────────────────────────── Starting ───────────────────────────────────────╮
│ PENTESTAGENT - Non-interactive Mode                                                    │
│                                                                                        │
│ Target: http://127.0.0.1:5000                                                          │
│ Model: openai/qwen3.6-27b-fable                                                        │
│ Mode: Agent                                                                            │
│ Runtime: Local                                                                         │
│ Max loops: 25                                                                          │
│ Task: Sweep notes, per-phase reports, phase logs and artifacts on disk for every       │
│ finding produced so far, normalise them into one list, and record them to notes so the │
│ next phase receives a complete input. Gathers only - does not test, does not chase new │
│ leads.                                                                                 │
│                                                                                        │
│ Phase: Read every channel                                                              │
│ Objective: Collect what previous phases produced, including the material that no       │
│ automated step reads.                                                                  │
│ Techniques:                                                                            │
│   1. Read the saved notes for this workspace first - that is the structured record,    │
│ and it is usually incomplete.                                                          │
│   2. Read the per-phase reports produced so far. Each phase writes its own report;     │
│ later phases do not read them.                                                         │
│   3. READ THE PHASE LOGS. This is the channel nothing else reads and the reason this   │
│ phase exists. Locate the log files for this assessment on disk and search them for     │
│ evidence that was stated but never recorded: recovered values, decoded output, tokens  │
│ and credentials, status codes that changed, headers observed to be missing, error      │
│ text, and any conclusion the assistant narrated about combining two things.            │
│   4. Pay particular attention to narration panels - the assistant frequently states a  │
│ completed chain or a confirmed result in prose without ever recording it as a finding. │
│ Those statements are findings and must be captured.                                    │
│   5. List the artifacts on disk (retrieved binaries, decompiled or unpacked source,    │
│ captured responses) and note their paths so later phases reuse them rather than        │
│ fetching again.                                                                        │
│                                                                                        │
│ Phase: Normalise into one list                                                         │
│ Objective: One entry per distinct issue, deduplicated and comparable.                  │
│ Techniques:                                                                            │
│   1. Produce one entry per distinct issue with: what it is, where it is (endpoint, or  │
│ file, type and member), the evidence observed, whether a control test was run and its  │
│ result, and the current proof level.                                                   │
│   2. Merge duplicates. Different phases routinely describe the same underlying issue   │
│ from different angles, and one issue split across several entries inflates the         │
│ apparent finding count.                                                                │
│   3. INCLUDE LOW-SEVERITY AND INFORMATIONAL OBSERVATIONS. Missing security headers,    │
│ permissive cross-origin policy, verbose errors and stack traces, exposed metadata and  │
│ version disclosure are routinely dropped between phases precisely because they are     │
│ individually minor. They belong in the report, and several of them are also inputs to  │
│ chaining.                                                                              │
│   4. Separate what has been demonstrated from what is asserted, and carry that         │
│ distinction forward rather than flattening it.                                         │
│   5. Build an explicit UNCOMBINED MATERIAL list: every secret, key, token, hash,       │
│ cookie or opaque value held, that has not yet been applied to anything. The next phase │
│ needs exactly this list, and material sitting unused is the most common missed         │
│ critical.                                                                              │
│                                                                                        │
│ Phase: Record to notes                                                                 │
│ Objective: Write the list down. This is the entire output of the phase - an unrecorded │
│ finding does not survive to the next one.                                              │
│ Techniques:                                                                            │
│   1. Record every normalised finding using the notes tool, one note per finding,       │
│ including its evidence and control result. Notes are the only channel later phases     │
│ reliably receive.                                                                      │
│   2. Record the UNCOMBINED MATERIAL list as its own note so the following phase can    │
│ act on it directly.                                                                    │
│   3. Do NOT re-test anything, do not attempt exploitation, and do not chase new leads  │
│ here. If something looks promising but unproven, record it as a lead with the evidence │
│ that makes it one and move on - the phases after this one will act on it.              │
│   4. Finish as soon as everything is recorded. This phase has a small budget by        │
│ design; spending it on investigation starves the phases that do the work.              │
│                                                                                        │
│                                                                                        │
